│  is the **recent swing low** (the low of the pullback that the cross         │
│  bounced from). This code never looks at it. Result: the stop is placed at   │
│  `entry − 1.5·ATR` regardless of whether the structural low is 0.3·ATR or    │
│  3·ATR away. So you get two failure modes:                                   │
│  - If the structural swing low is *closer* than 1.5·ATR, you are taking      │
│  **more risk than structure demands**.                                       │
│  - If the structural swing low is *farther* than 1.5·ATR, your stop sits     │
│  **inside** the structure and gets tagged by normal noise before the thesis  │
│  can play out.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  ### Flaw B — Wicks stop you out (HIGH/LOW vs. CLOSE-based stops)            │
│  The exit check:                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  current_low = df['Low'].iloc[i]                                             │
│  if current_low <= sl:   -> LOSER                                            │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  It compares the bar **low** (wick) to the stop. On 5-minute SPY/QQQ this    │
│  is exactly where stop-hunt wicks live. A purely ATR stop combined with      │
│  wick-based triggering is the classic "stopped out then the trade runs to    │
│  full TP2" failure. A structure-aware stop (below a swing low, with a small  │
│  ATR buffer beyond the *low*, not the close) would not have this leakage.    │
│  **Currently a single wick one tick below `entry − 1.5·ATR` kills a trade    │
│  that may be perfectly valid.**                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  ### Flaw C — No volatility-regime awareness                                 │
│  `ATR` is a single 14-bar rolling mean, and the stop is exactly `1.5·ATR`    │
│  at all times. There is no regime filter (e.g., ATR percentile, ATR          │
│  expansion/contraction, session volatility, VIX). During a low-volatility    │
│  lunch lull a `1.5·ATR` stop is a tiny, easily-grabbed level; during an      │
│  open spike the same multiple is enormous. Because it never adjusts to       │
│  structure, the stop is either too tight or too loose purely as a function   │
│  of the indicator value — not of where price actually broke down.            │
│                                                                              │
